    "body": "Sure, I understand the _how_ to find out when an element is \"valid\" (the \"Contexts in which this element can be used\" sections in each element), what I was looking for is a higher level of guidance/confirmation.\r\n\r\nFor example, while the `<table>` element is a valid anywhere in there is additional text around like \"Authors are encouraged to...\" and \"Tables must not be used as layout aids.\", etc. Similarly, The `<p>` or `<div>` elements are valid in a lot of places, but there is text around it saying they should not be used when a more specific element is appropriate.\r\n\r\nGiven that there used to be a text calling out that it _can be_ appropriate to use controls outside of the context of a `<form>`, and that text no longer exists, it is a bit ambiguous whether that is now _discouraged_ or what, especially that there is a [big section of non-normative text](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#forms) discussing the topic and these use cases are not mentioned at all.\r\n\r\nThe specific thing that came up was that we had a discussion on our project where someone pointed out we used a `<select>` element outside of a form and that is invalid/bad practice/semantically wrong/inaccessible. I am pretty sure that is not true, and I figured it would be easily answered by referencing the spec. Whether it's \"valid\" or not was easily settled, in terms of \"Contexts in which this element can be used\", but whether it is a \"bad idea\", or whether the spec even has an opinion on that, was somewhat harder to interpret given the use case was not mentioned in the otherwise very detailed introductory text, plus the omission/change between HTML 4.01 and HTML 5+.\r\n\r\nIMO, since there is already so much non-normative introductory text around forms, a sentence pointing out there exists some non-form use cases of \"form\" controls wouldn't hurt. It's about the use cases/appropriateness, not the structural validity of the document, so it doesn't duplicate the new format, and there are IMO plenty of other precedences of that in the spec already.\r\n\r\nSecondary to (and maybe separately form) that, the spec just doesn't do a very good job of defining semantically what a `<form>` is or is not. My intuition is that it has to be logically \"submittable\", whether there is literally a submit button and whether it is using the native/default submit behavior or not, but that was kind of hard to validate using the spec.\r\n\r\nThe closest thing it said was:\r\n\r\n> The form element represents a hyperlink that can be manipulated through a collection of form-associated elements, some of which can represent editable values that can be submitted to a server for processing.\r\n\r\nWhat felt like it's at the same time too specific (\"to a server\") and too vague (\"represents\" a hyperlink) at the same time. For example, if a group of controls exists to filter data that exists only on the client (e.g. filter/search controls for the TodoMVC), should it not be wrapped in a `<form>` tag?",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
