    "updated_at": "2020-09-25T00:12:11Z",
    "body": "Well, I can try to summarize. I think there are a couple of related issues:\r\n\r\n1. The general [Forms](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/forms.html#forms) section – probably somewhere in \"4.10.1 Introduction\" – should explicitly affirm/acknowledge that there are use cases for using \"controls\" to build user interfaces outside of a \"form\".\r\n\r\n2. Sure, specific examples for each individual control suggesting what _else_ they can be used for other than in a \"form\", would be great, and I am happy to provide them, but it wasn't really what I am after originally.\r\n\r\n3. The semantic meaning of the `<form>` element could be improved.\r\n\r\n* * *\r\n\r\nThe first issue is what I am mostly after here. I am happy to split the rest to separate issues.\r\n\r\nIt is unfortunate that there isn't a separate \"Controls\" section separate from \"Forms\" as a parent for all the input types, select, button, etc, but I think it's understandable. Absent of that, the logical place to describe that would be a sentence in the Forms Introduction section since that's the closest shared parent.\r\n\r\nIt doesn't have to go into details, just to point out the fact that this section isn't necessarily exclusively about `<form>`s, despite the title. This is unlike the similarly structured [Tabular Data](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/tables.html#tables) section, which _is_ exclusively about `<table>`s.\r\n\r\nAll I am suggesting we add back is a sentence along the lines of:\r\n\r\n> 4.10.1 Introduction\r\n> \r\n> _This section is non-normative._\r\n>\r\n> A form is a component of a web page that has form controls, such as text, buttons, checkboxes, range, or color picker controls. A user can interact with such a form, providing data that can then be sent to the server for further processing (e.g. returning the results of a search or calculation). No client-side scripting is needed in many cases, though an API is available so that scripts can augment the user experience or use forms for purposes other than submitting data to a server.\r\n>\r\n> **+++ This section covers both forms and form controls. While form controls are generally used to build forms, they can also be useful for building user interfaces outside of a form. +++**\r\n>\r\n> Writing a form consists of several steps, which can be performed in any order: writing the user interface, implementing the server-side processing, and configuring the user interface to communicate with the server.\r\n\r\nHappy to discuss and iterate on the exact wording, making it less verbose, working it into the existing prose, etc. But I do think we lost a semantically meaningful and important sentence from the HTML 4.01 era, that is is no longer found in the specs and is not otherwise covered by the new structure, and I disagree that it's just a matter of needing examples.",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
